## Characterization Techniques
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of samples were obtained on a Rigaku Miniflex-II diffractometer by using $\operatorname{Cu}K\alpha$ radiation $\left(\lambda=\right.$ $1.54178{\mathrm{~\AA}})$ in a $2\theta$ range of $5\mathrm{-}60^{\circ}$ . The results of elemental analyses (EA) were recorded using a German Elementary Vario EL III instrument. Thermogravimetric curves were recorded in a $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere on a thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) instrument with a heating rate of $10~\mathrm{K/\bar{min}}$ . The optical adsorption spectra were measured with a Shimadzu 2600 UV/vis spectrometer. Energydispersive spectroscopy (EDS) and elemental distribution mapping were performed with a JEOL JSM-6700F scanning electron microscope. The morphologies of prepared samples were examined with a field emission scanning electron microscope (SEM, HITACHI FE-SEM SU8010). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted on an ESCALAB 250Xi type X-ray photoelectron spectrometer. Inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) was performed on an ICAP-7400 system to measure the sodium, potassium, calcium, and magnesium concentrations. The concentrations of cesium and strontium were measured by inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) on a XSerise II instrument. 1 and 2 were irradiated with γ-rays at total doses of
100 $1.1\mathrm{kGy/h}$ for $95\mathrm{{h}}$ ) and $200\ensuremath{\mathrm{kG}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{y}}$ $\langle1.1\mathrm{kGy/h}$ for $181.8\mathrm{h}$ ) using a $^{60}\dot{\mathrm{Co}}$ irradiation source (two million curies) provided by the Detection Center of Suzhou CNNC Huadong Radiation Co., Ltd., China.
## Ion Exchange Experiments
To aqueous solutions of CsCl with the different concentrations of ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\prime}_{10\ \mathrm{mL}})$ was added the ground polycrystalline powder of 1 or 2 (10 ${\mathrm{mg}}, $ ). The mixture was kept under magnetic stirring at RT for $^{10\mathrm{~h},}$ filtered by the $0.22\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ Millipore filter on the syringe, then diluted with dilute nitric acid to meet the concentration range of the test instrument. The concentrations of ions were determined by ICP-OES or ICP-MS. More details about the ion exchange experiments are provided in the Supporting Information.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## Crystal Structures
SCXRD shows that 1 crystallizes in the monoclinic space group $P2_{\mathrm{1}}/c$ . Its asymmetric unit contains one $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ ion, two aip2− ligands, one DMF moleucle, one $[\mathrm{(CH_{3})_{2}N H_{2}}]^{+}$ ion, and one lattice water (Figure S1a). Each $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ ion is sevencoordinated by six oxygen atoms from three chelating carboxyl groups and one oxygen atom from one monodentate carboxyl group, giving rise to a distorted $\left[\mathrm{InO}_{7}\right]$ polyhedron (Figure S2a). The lengths of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{In-O}}$ bonds range from 2.122(3) to 2.531(3) Å (Table S2). A long $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{In-O}}$ distance $\left(\mathrm{In}(1){-}\mathrm{O}(6)=\right.$ $2.731(4)\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ is not considered. Each $\left[\mathrm{InO}_{7}\right].$ polyhedron connects to four adjacent $\left[\mathrm{InO}_{7}\right]$ polyhedrons through four $\mathsf{a i p}^{2-}$ ligands to form an anionic layer of $\left[\operatorname{In}(\mathrm{aip})_{2}\right]_{n}^{n-}$ with the square-like window of $13.30\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times1\mathring{3}.96\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Figure 1a). The In− $_\mathrm{L-In}$ (where L is the center of benzene ring on the aip2− ligand linking two In atoms) angle is $112.67^{\circ}$ , resulting in a waved anionic layer of $\left[\operatorname{In}(\mathrm{aip})_{2}\right]_{n}^{n-}$ (Figures 1b, S3a, and S4a). TOPOS analysis indicates the anionic layer is a sql topological type when the $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ ions are considered as four-connected nodes and the aip2− ligands are considered as bridging linkers (Figure S3b).
The layers are stacked along the $c$ -axis in an $A B$ fashion (Figure 1b and c). The adjacent layers are embedded in each other, and the amino groups of the $\mathsf{a i p}^{2-}$ ligands point to the interlayer space. The shortest $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{In-In}}$ distance of the adjacent anionic layers in 1 is $7.74\mathrm{~\AA}.$ The protonated dimethylamine cations from the in situ decomposition of DMF are located between the layers, compensating the negative charge of the anionic network.25,37 Furthermore, $[(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}]^{+}$ cations interact with DMF and lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules through hydrogen bonds (Figure 1a and Table S3), which further interact with anionic frameworks via hydrogen bonds. Thus, layers are further linked by hydrogen bonds to form a 3D supramolecular structure. To a certain degree, abundant hydrogen bonding enhances the skeleton stability of 1. Although it has been reported that a structurally similar compound $\big[\big(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\big)_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big]\big[\mathrm{\bar{In}(a i p)}_{2}\big]\big\}{\cdot}x\mathrm{G}$ ( $\mathbf{\bar{G}=}$ guest) (space group $\boldsymbol{P}\boldsymbol{\overline{{1}}}$ ) could adsorb methylene blue (MB) by the ion exchange method,43 radionuclide remediation by a thuslayered framework has not been documented.
2 belongs to the monoclinic space group $C2/c$ . Its asymmetric unit contains half a formula unit (Figure S1d). Compared with the seven-coordination of $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ in 1, the $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ ion in 2 is coordinated by eight oxygen atoms from four $\scriptstyle\operatorname{hip}^{2-}$ ligands in a bidentate chelating fashion to form a $\left[\mathrm{InO}_{8}\right]$ polyhedron. The adjacent $\left[\mathrm{InO}_{8}\right]$ polyhedra are linked by $\mathrm{{\hat{h}i p}}^{\cdot2-}$ ligands to form a square-like window with a size of 13.25 $\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times14$ . 67 Å (Figure S5a), which is further extended by edgesharing to form an anionic layer structure of $\left[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{hip})_{2}\right]_{n}^{n-}$ (Figure S5b). The $_\mathrm{In-L-In}$ (where L is the center of benzene ring on the $\displaystyle\mathrm{hip}^{2-}$ ligand linking two In atoms) angle is $127.661(1)^{\circ}$ in 2 as opposed to that of $112.671(4)^{\circ}$ in 1, resulting in a more flatter anionic layer in 2 (Figure S4a and d). The hydroxyl groups of $\displaystyle\mathrm{hip}^{2-}$ ligands point to the interlayer space similar to that of amino groups in 1 (Figure S5c). The anionic layers in 2 are also stacked in the AB fashion. The shortest $\mathrm{In-In}$ distance of the adjacent anionic layers in 2 is $7.53\mathrm{\AA},$ , which is closer than $7.74\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in 1. The negative charge of the $\left[\operatorname{In}(\operatorname{hip})_{2}\right]_{n}n^{-}$ network is balanced with $\bar{\mathrm{[(CH_{3})_{2}N H_{2}]^{+}}}$ cations, which interact with the anionic layers via $\mathrm{N-H\cdotsO}$ hydrogen bonds (Table S5 and Figure S5a). DMF and lattice water molecules are also distributed between the layers (Figure S5d). 2 has been reported before by Yong et al.,44 where it showed outstanding robustness in a wide range of $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ levels and excellent adsorption for positive charge dyes, but it has not been applied to radionuclide remediation. 1 and 2 have the anionic layers, exchangeable $[(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}]^{+}$ cations, and carboxyl functional groups. These structural features will provide convenience for ion exchange between chargebalancing cations and $C s^{+}$ ions.
The phase-pure crystals of 1 were obtained without manual selection as verified by PXRD, and the acid and alkali resistances of 1 were evaluated. 1 retained its layered structure after being immersed in aqueous solutions with various $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ values (in the range from 2.98 to 11.05) for $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ (Figure S7a). Furthermore, the leaching percentages of In for 1 were lower than $0.2\%$ in the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ range of 2.98−12.06 (Figure S7b and Table S6). These results demonstrate that 1 exhibits a good stability in a wide $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ range.
## Structural Transformation
Fortunately, we obtained single crystals for the $C s^{+}$ adsorbed products of 1 and 2, namely 1-Cs and 2-Cs, respectively. SCXRD analyses clearly reveal that the asymmetric unit of 1- Cs contains one formula unit (Figure S1b). Although both space groups of the pristine 1 and 1-Cs are in the space group $P2_{1}/c,$ the lengths of crystallographic $a\mathrm{-}$ and $c$ -axis are reduced from 10.0110(15) and 14.956(3) Å in the pristine 1 to 9.8620(3) and 14.3580(5) $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ in 1-Cs, respectively (Table S1). The coordination mode of the $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ ion varies from sevencoordination in 1 to eight-coordination in 1-Cs (Figure S2d), originating from the carboxyl groups of one $\mathrm{aip}^{2-}$ ligand changing from the monodentate chelating mode in 1 to the bidentate chelating mode in 1-Cs (Figure S2f). The square-like window in the anionic layer shrunk from $13.30\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times\mathring{13}.96\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in 1 to $13.49\mathrm{~\AA~}\times\mathrm{~}13.53\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in 1-Cs, which was induced by the substitution of $[(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}]^{+}$ and DMF with ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules, respectively; additionally, ${{\mathrm{C}}{s}^{+}}$ ions were trapped between the layers in 1-Cs (Figures 1a, d, and e). The size of the In−L−In angle increased from $112.67^{\circ}$ in the pristine 1 to $113.21^{\circ}$ in 1-Cs (Figures S4a and b). The ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ ion is surrounded by three water molecules and six oxygen atoms of the six $\mathsf{C O O^{-}}$ groups from six $\mathrm{aip}^{2-}$ ligands to give a $\left[\mathrm{CsO}_{9}\right]$ polyhedron in 1-Cs. The $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Cs-O}}$ distances range from 3.001(4) to 3.311(4) Å (Table S2). Thus, ${{\mathrm{C}}{s}}^{+}$ incorporation into the original interlayer of 1 results in a significant contraction of the ${\big[}{\mathrm{In}}{\big(}{\mathrm{aip}}{\big)}_{2}{\big]}_{n}^{n-}$ interlayer space and is accompanied by the departure of $[\mathrm{(CH_{3})_{2}N H_{2}}]^{+}$ ions and DMF molecules. The shortening of the shortest $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{In-In}}$ distance from $7.74\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in 1 to $7.38\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in 1-Cs (Figures 1c and f), which is mainly attributed to the strong interaction of $\mathbf{Cs}{\cdots}{\mathbf{C}}{\mathbf{O}}{\mathbf{O}}^{-}$ groups, reflects this and indicates that 1 possess structural flexibility. Importantly, these structural transformation analyses confirm the successful entrance of ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ ions by the ion exchange mode and clearly reveal the ${{\mathrm{C}}{s}^{+}}$ uptake mechanism of 1.
2 undergoes a similar ion exchange process for ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ uptake compared to that for 1. SCXRD shows that the space group of 2-Cs still remains $C2/{\mathrm{c}}.$ . Its asymmetric unit contains half a $\bar{\mathrm{In}}^{3+}$ ion, one $\displaystyle\mathrm{hip}^{2-}$ ligand, half a cation $(\mathrm{Cs}_{0.36}(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O})_{0.14},\mathrm{Cs}_{0.36}=$ $\mathrm{Csl}_{0.34}+\mathrm{CslB}_{0.02})$ , and two and a half water molecules (Figure S1e). The $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ ion is still eight-coordinated, forming a $\left[\mathrm{InO}_{8}\right]$ polyhedron. It is evident that the lengths of the crystallographic a- and $b$ -axes change from 14.6706(14) and 13.2520(13) Å in pristine 2 to 13.0956(4) and $14.77(4)$ in 2-Cs, respectively (Table S1). This change is accompanied by the change of the window sizes from $1\Bar{3}.25\Bar{\mathrm{~A~}}\times\Bar{14}.67\Bar{\mathrm{~A~}}$ in pristine 2 to $13.10\mathrm{~\AA~}\times14.77\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in 2-Cs (Figures S5a and e). The In−L−In angle is basically unchanged (Figures S4d and e). ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ ions are trapped between the $\left[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{hip})_{2}\right]_{n}n^{-}$ layers (Figure S5f), which are surrounded by four oxygen atoms from four $\mathsf{C O O^{-}}$ groups and four water molecules to form $\left[\mathrm{CsO}_{8}\right]$ polyhedra. The $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Cs-O}}$ distances are in the range of $2.78(3)-$ 3.62(2) Å (Table S4). The strong interactions between ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ and oxygen atoms of $\mathsf{C O O^{-}}$ groups from $\left[\operatorname{In}(\operatorname{hip})_{2}\right]_{n}^{n-}$ layers lead to a shortening of the $\mathbf{In}(\mathbf{hip})_{2}]_{n}^{\mathfrak{n}-}$ interlayer space, with the closest $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{In-In}}$ distance of adjacent layers changing from $7.53\mathrm{\AA}$ in 2 to $7.18\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in 2-Cs (Figure $S5\mathrm{g}{\dot{}} $ ). These changes fully confirm the structural flexibility of 2 and clarify the ${\mathbf{C}}{\mathbf{s}}^{+}$ uptake mechanism of 2.
Strikingly, the absorbed ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}_{\mathbf{S}}}}^{+}$ ions of 1-Cs can be easily eluted by a KCl solution. The SCXRD, EA, and TGA analyses of eluted product imply that its formula is $\mathrm{K}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{3}\mathrm{In}\big(\mathrm{aip}\big)_{2}.$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1-K). 1-K also crystallizes in the monoclinic space group $P2_{\mathrm{1}}/c$ (Table S1) with one formula in the asymmetric unit (Figure S1c). The connection types of the $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ ion and the aip2− ligand in 1-K are the same as those in compound 1-Cs. The $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ion is surrounded by six oxygen atoms from $C00^{-}$ functional groups and three water molecules (Figure $\mathrm{{\calS}2g}^{\mathrm{~~}}$ ). It is distinct that the size of the square-like window changes from 13.49 $\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times13.53\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in 1-Cs to $13.40\mathrm{~\AA~}\times13.63\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in $\mathbf{1-K},$ and $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions are also trapped between the layers in 1-K (Figures 1d, ${\bf g},$ and h). The interlayered $\mathrm{In-In}$ distance is further shortened to $7.13\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Figure 1i) due to the interaction between $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions and $\left[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{aip})_{2}\right]_{n}^{n-}$ layers via $\mathrm{~o~}$ atoms of carboxyl groups from $\mathsf{a i p}^{2-}$ ligands. The results show that 1-Cs can be easily eluted in the presence of high concentrations of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions, further demonstrating the robustness and flexibility of the $\left[\operatorname{In}(\mathrm{aip})_{2}\right]_{n}^{n-}$ layer framework in 1. Note that the clear ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ elution mechanism was first revealed to be the replacement of ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ with $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ from the view of structural chemistry for MOFs materials.
## Characterizations for Ion-Exchanged Products
The ion exchange of $[(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}]^{+}$ and $C s^{+}$ in 1 and 2 was confirmed by various characterization methods, including ICPOES, ICP-MS, EDS, SEM, and XPS. Photographs of crystals for 1, 1-Cs, 1-K, 2 and 2-Cs are depicted in Figure 2a and e. The color of the samples before and after the ${\mathrm{Cs}}^{\mathrm{\Delta}+}$ ion exchange of 1 as well as the eluted product 1-K remain brown, which is consistent with the results of UV−vis spectra; that is, the optical adsorption edges of 1 $\left(3.06\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , 1-Cs $(3.11\mathrm{eV})$ , and 1- K $\left(3.07~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ are close (Figure $\mathrm{{\calS}9a}$ ). Photographs of crystals of 2 and 2-Cs obviously show that the crystal shape changes from the block-like shape of pristine 2 to the flake-like shape or microcrystals of 2-Cs (Figure 2e). The optical adsorption edge of 2 is $3.55~\mathrm{eV}$ and that of 2-Cs shows a blue-shift to $3.64~\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S9b). SEM images show that the crystal of 1 has smooth surfaces. 1-Cs retains the pristine shapes but its surface is lightly eroded, while the surface of 1-K becomes rougher than that of 1-Cs (Figures 2b, $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{c}},$ and ${\bf d},$ respectively). Compared with the pristine 2, the surfaces of 2-Cs have obvious cracks (Figures 2f and $\mathbf{g},$ respectively).
The results of EDS and elemental distribution maps of ionexchanged products verify that ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ ions entered the samples of 1 and 2 (Figure S10) and were homogeneously distributed in the samples (Figures 3a and b, respectively). The $C s^{+}$ exchanged products exhibit evident characteristic peaks of $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ for ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ at 724.5 and $738.6~\mathrm{eV}$ (Figures 3c and d) for 1, respectively, and at 724.7 and $738.6~\mathrm{eV}$ (Figures 3e and f) for 2 in the XPS survey spectra.45 The peaks at 399.3 and $402.1~\mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to N 1s of the amino groups46 and dimethylamine cations in ${\mathbf{1}},^{29}$ respectively (Figure S11a). Contrasted with the pristine 1 and 2, the characteristic peaks for N 1s of the dimethylamine cation for 1-Cs and 2-Cs at 402.1 and $402.3\mathrm{~\eV}_{;}$ , respectively, almost disappear (Figure S11). This evidence suggests that ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ ions have successfully entered the structures of 1 and 2 by the ion exchange mode, accompanied by the departure of $[\mathrm{(CH_{3})_{2}N H_{2}}]^{+}$ cations.
## Kinetic Studies
The effects of the contact time for ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ ion exchange with 1 and 2 were investigated. The kinetic curves show that the concentration of ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ steeply decreases with time (Figures 4a and b). The removal rate of ${{\mathrm{C}}{s}^{+}}$ $\mathbf{\mathcal{R}}^{\mathrm{C}s},$ , equation S1) by 1 rapidly reached $92.92\%$ within $1~\mathrm{min}$ and then gradually reached equilibrium within $\textsf{S m i n}$ (Table S7). $\boldsymbol{R}^{\mathrm{Cs}}$ of 2 can reach $89.23\%$ within $1~\mathrm{\min}$ . As the time increased, the adsorption process of ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ ions by 2 gradually reached equilibrium within $10\ \mathrm{min}$ (Table S8). The ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ adsorption kinetics of 1 and 2 are significantly faster than those of other adsorption materials, such as $\big[\big(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\big)_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big]\big[\mathrm{UO}_{2}\big(\mathrm{L}_{2}\big)\big]$ · $0\cdot5\bar{\textrm{D M F}}\cdot1\ 5\textrm{H}_{2}\textrm{O}$ $\left(\begin{array}{l l l l}{2}&{0}&{}&{}&{\mathrm{~m~i~n~}}\end{array}\right.$ ) , ${\dot{2}}^{-}{\dot{S}}$ $\big[\big(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\big)_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big]_{4}\big[\big(\mathrm{UO}_{2}\big)_{4}\big(\mathrm{TBAPy}\big)_{3}\big]\cdot18\mathrm{DMF}\cdot17\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ $(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{TBAPy}~=~1,3,6,8$ -tetrakis( $p$ -benzoic acid)pyrene, 30 min),47 FJSM-InMOF $(3\ \mathrm{h}),^{29}$ zeolite A $\left(90{-}120\ \mathrm{\min}\right)$ ,48 KMS-2 $\left(10\mathrm{-}15\mathrm{h}\right),^{2}\mathrm{H\mathrm{-}C S T\left(1\mathrm{~h}\right),^{49}}$ and K-SGU-45 $(15\mathrm{h})$ .50 The rapid kinetics of 1 and 2 toward ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ ions are mainly ascribed to the strong interactions between $\mathsf{C O O^{-}}$ groups and ${\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}$ ion and , the flexibility of layered structures with open windows. The kinetic data were fitted with pseudo-first-order (equation S2) and pseudo-second-order kinetics models (equation S3). Both kinetics data were found be fit better by the pseudo-second-order kinetic model, with high correlation coefficients $R^{2}$ of 0.99997 for 1 and 0.99994 for 2 (Figure S12).
