## Irradiation Resistance
Considering that materials will be exposed to a strong radiation environment to handle the radioactive waste, the radiation stabilities of 1 and 2 were investigated. PXRD patterns of samples before and after radiation show that the original structures of 1 and 2 were maintained after 100 and $200\mathrm{kGy}\gamma$ irradiation (Figure S13). This demonstrates that they possess excellent radiation-resistant properties. We also tested the ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ ion exchange properties of two compounds before and after irradiation (Figure 6a and Table S19). $\dot{R}^{\mathrm C s}$ of 1 before and after irradiation can remain in the range of $88-92\%$ under different initial concentrations of ${\mathbf{C}}s^{+}$ , and $\mathrm{\check{K}}_{\mathrm{d}}^{\mathrm{Cs}}$ are more than $10^{3}\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{g}$ . Similarly, $\boldsymbol{R}^{\mathrm{Cs}}$ and $K_{\mathrm{d}}^{\mathrm{Cs}}$ values of 2 before and after irradiation remain almost unchanged. These results demonstrate that irradiation did not affect the ${{\bf C}}s^{+}$ adsorption performance of 1 and 2, further indicating their potential applications in radioactive waste treatment.
## Elution and Reuse
It is worth noting that the ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ exchanged products of 1 and 2 can be easily eluted with a $0.3{\bf M}$ KCl solution. EDS analyses of both eluted products confirm that ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ ions could be entirely replaced by $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions (Figure S14). Elemental distribution mapping reveals that $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ions show the homogeneous distribution in the eluted productions (Figure S15a and b). We took 1-Cs as a representative to study its elution in detail. The XPS spectrum of 1-K exhibits characteristic $\mathsf{2p}$ peaks for $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ at 293.3 and $296.0\mathrm{~\eV}$ (Figures S15c and d). The characteristic peaks of ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ disappear after elution, indicating the successful elution of ${{\mathrm{C}}{s}^{+}}$ . The $\boldsymbol{R}^{\mathrm{C}s}$ value of 1-K can remained in the range of $84.89-87.41\%$ under different initial concentrations of ${\dot{\mathbf{C}}}{\mathbf{s}}^{+}$ after two adsorption−elution process recycles. Even in the third cycle, the $\boldsymbol{R}^{\dot{\mathbb{C}}s}$ value can still reach $80.48\%$ (Figure 6b and Table S20). Additionally, PXRD shows 1-K could still maintain the crystallinity and stability of its layered framework after three adsorption−elution cycles (Figure S16a-d). EDS analyses of eluted products confirm that ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ ions could be entirely eluted by $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions (Figure S16e and 16f). Therefore, easy-to-operate elution and regeneration highlight the potential of the current layered compounds for the enrichment and recycle of cesium.
## Mechanism on ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{s}}^{+}$ Uptake
Although MOFs materials have been reported for the uptake of ${{\mathrm{C}}{s}^{+}}$ ion s,25−29 it is still a challenge to deeply understand the ion exchange mechanism at an atomic level due to the worse crystallinity of MOF materials after adsorbing ions. In particular, it is still rare for a layered MOF to be used for the uptake of ${{\mathrm{C}}{s}^{+}}$ . 1 and 2 were prepared using the derivatives of isophthalic acid and exhibited excellent ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ uptake performance. The derivatives of isophthalic acid are powerful assistants for constructing layered structures and bring structural advantages for ion exchange, such as functional groups, adjustable interlayer spacing, and flexible frameworks. The charge balancing agent, $\mathsf{\tilde{I}}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathsf{I}^{+}$ cations with an easy exchangeable ability, are useful for directing anionic frameworks. Impressively, 1 and 2 are robust enough for single crystals to be obtained after the exchange of ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ ions. SC-SC structural transformations clearly reveal that ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ ions are embedded in the interlayer through collaborative interactions with the anionic layers. It is crucial for the ion exchange process that there are the strong interactions between $C s^{+}$ ions and $\mathsf{C O O^{-}}$ groups from anionic layers. Simultaneously, XPS analyses confirm the disappearance or absence of N 1s of the dimethylamine cations in both ${\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}$ exchanged products, indicating the easy escape of $\mathrm{[Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{]}^{+}$ cations. Moreover, compared with the pristine compounds, the shortest $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{In-In}}$ distances between adjacent layers and the square-like window sizes within the layers of 1-Cs, 1-K and 2-Cs changed, confirming structural flexibility and accommodation ability of the current layered compounds. In other words, the excellent ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ ion exchange performances of 1 and 2 are attributed to the strong interactions between functional $\mathsf{C O O^{-}}$ groups and ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ ions, easily exchangeable $\big[\big(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\big)_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big]^{+}$ , and flexible and robust anionic layers with open windows. In the case of MOF materials, the ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ adsorption−elution mechanisms are clearly unveiled for the first time at the molecular level.
## CONCLUSION
In conclusion, two layered MOFs materials with wide $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ durabilities and excellent radiation stabilities were prepared using the derivatives of isophthalic acid. 1 and 2 exhibit not only high adsorption capacities for ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}{\boldsymbol{s}}^{+}$ ions but also ultrafast removal abilities for ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}_{\mathbf{S}}}}^{+}$ ions. Particularly, both compounds can selectively remove ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ in the presence of interfering ions and achieve $\mathrm{\dot{C}s^{+}}/\mathrm{Sr}^{2+}$ separation in a wide range of $\mathrm{{\bar{S}r/C s}}$ molar ratios. Additionally, the ${\mathrm{C}}s^{+}$ exchange products of 1 can be regenerated and recycled by a cost-affordable method. More significantly, this work systematically reveals the $C s^{+}$ uptake and elution mechanisms of layered MOF materials from the view of structural chemistry, with the fascinating structural transformation from layered structures to three-dimensional frameworks. This work not only develops layered MOF materials as radionuclide scavengers but also deepens the understanding of the relationship between the structure of materials and the property for the removal of a radionuclide. Eventually, this work provides insight into the design of new ion exchange materials for application in the treatment of radioactive contamination.
